1. What is Ansible Galaxy?

Ansible Galaxy is a community platform where Ansible users can share, find, download, and reuse automation code.

When working with Ansible, we often need to perform common tasks such as:

Installing Docker
Installing Nginx
Installing Apache
Configuring MySQL
Creating users
Configuring monitoring tools
Setting up web servers

We can write all these tasks ourselves, but this can take a lot of time.

Ansible Galaxy provides ready-made automation content created by other Ansible users and organizations.

The two important types of content are:

a) Roles

A Role is a reusable collection of Ansible files and tasks used to perform a particular job.

For example, a Docker role may contain tasks to:

Install Docker
       ↓
Configure Docker
       ↓
Start Docker service
       ↓
Enable Docker service

Instead of writing all these tasks ourselves, we can download an existing Docker role and use it.

b) Collections

A Collection is a larger package of Ansible content.

It can contain:

Roles
Modules
Plugins
Playbooks
Other Ansible content

So, you can think of it like this:

Ansible Galaxy
      |
      +---- Roles
      |
      +---- Collections

For beginners, Roles are the easiest place to start understanding Galaxy.

2.  Ansible Galaxy vs Docker Hub

Ansible Galaxy and Docker Hub are not the same thing, but the basic idea of reusing existing content is similar.

a) Docker Hub

Docker Hub provides ready-made Docker images.

Docker Hub
    |
    +--- nginx image
    +--- ubuntu image
    +--- mysql image
    +--- redis image

For example:

docker pull nginx

This downloads the Nginx Docker image.

b) Ansible Galaxy

Ansible Galaxy provides reusable Ansible content.

Ansible Galaxy
    |
    +--- Docker role
    +--- Nginx role
    +--- Apache role
    +--- MySQL role

=> For example:

ansible-galaxy role install <namespace>.<role>

This downloads an Ansible role to your local machine.

=> Simple comparison

a) Docker Hub
    ↓
Provides Docker images
    ↓
docker pull nginx


b) Ansible Galaxy
    ↓
Provides Ansible roles/collections
    ↓
ansible-galaxy role install ...

So the main idea is:

Docker Hub → Reuse Docker images

Ansible Galaxy → Reuse Ansible automation

3. Why do we use Ansible Galaxy?

The biggest reason is reuse.

Imagine you are working in a company and your team needs to install and configure Nginx on 50 servers.

You could write all the tasks yourself:

- Install Nginx
- Copy configuration
- Copy website files
- Start Nginx
- Enable Nginx
- Configure firewall

But someone may have already created a good reusable role for this purpose.

Instead of starting from zero, you can use that role.

=> Main benefits
a) Saves time

We don't have to write common automation from scratch.

b) Reusability

Once a role is created, it can be used in multiple projects.

c) Keeps playbooks clean

Instead of putting hundreds of tasks inside one playbook, we can move the actual automation into roles.

The playbook becomes simple:

- hosts: webservers
  roles:
    - nginx

The complicated work is inside the role.

d) Learn from existing roles

As a beginner, Galaxy roles can help you understand how experienced Ansible users organize their automation.

You can look at the role's:

tasks/
handlers/
templates/
defaults/
vars/
meta/

and understand how they have designed it.

e) Share your own roles

We can also create our own roles and share them with the community.

4. Should we blindly use a Galaxy role?

No.

This is very important, especially in a company environment.

A role downloaded from Galaxy is created by someone else. We should understand what it does before using it.

For example, suppose you find:

someuser.docker

Don't immediately install it on production servers.

First check:

a) Author

Who created the role?

Is it an individual developer or a trusted organization?

b) GitHub repository

Check whether the role has a GitHub repository.

Look at:

README
Issues
Documentation
Source code

c) Supported operating systems

A role may support:

Ubuntu
Debian
RHEL
CentOS
Amazon Linux

d) Version

Check which version of the software the role supports.

For example:

Docker 28
Docker 29

The role may have been written for an older version.

