5. The ansible-galaxy Command

The command-line tool used to work with Ansible Galaxy content is:

ansible-galaxy

To see available options:

ansible-galaxy --help

You can also check its version:

ansible-galaxy --version

6. Installing an Ansible Role

One of the important commands is:

ansible-galaxy role install <namespace>.<role>

For example:

ansible-galaxy role install bsmeding.docker

Here:

bsmeding.docker
     ↑
namespace + role

The command downloads the role to your local Ansible roles directory.

*********

namespace simply means the name/identity of the person or organization that published the role.

=> Why do we need a namespace?

Because many people can create a role with the same name.

For example:

john.docker
company.docker
abc.docker

All three could be Docker roles, but they come from different authors/namespaces.

Think of it like GitHub:

github.com/username/repository
          │        │
          │        └── Repository
          └── Username

Similarly, in Ansible Galaxy:

namespace.role
     │      │
     │      └── Role
     └── Author/Organization

So when you see:

ansible-galaxy role install bsmeding.docker

read it as:

Install the docker role published under the bsmeding namespace.

*******

7. What happens after installing a role?

Suppose we run:

ansible-galaxy role install bsmeding.docker

The role is downloaded to the local system.

You may see a directory structure similar to:

roles/
└── bsmeding.docker/
    ├── tasks/
    ├── handlers/
    ├── defaults/
    ├── vars/
    ├── templates/
    ├── files/
    └── meta/

Now the important point is:

We don't normally copy all those tasks into our playbook.

Instead, our playbook simply tells Ansible:

Use this role.

For example:

roles:
  - bsmeding.docker

Ansible then executes the tasks provided by that role.

8. Important Difference: Galaxy vs Role

Don't confuse these two.

Ansible Galaxy is the platform/ecosystem where Ansible content is shared.

Ansible Role is the reusable automation package.

Simple example:

Ansible Galaxy
      |
      +---- Role 1
      |
      +---- Role 2
      |
      +---- Role 3

So Galaxy can provide roles, while a role is the actual reusable automation.

9. Final Simple Understanding

You can remember Ansible Galaxy like this:

Ansible Galaxy
      ↓
Community platform for Ansible content
      ↓
Find reusable automation
      ↓
Download a Role / Collection
      ↓
Use it in your Playbook
      ↓
Automate your servers

=> And the complete flow is

Search Galaxy
      ↓
Find a suitable Role
      ↓
Check author + GitHub + documentation
      ↓
Check OS support + version + dependencies
      ↓
Install the Role
      ↓
Use Role in Playbook
      ↓
Run Playbook
      ↓
Servers get configured
